  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 PM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nFor the rest of the day, mainly widely scattered rain showers\nand possibly (10-20% chance only) an isolated non-severe \nthunderstorm are forecast this afternoon for inland northwest \nWisconsin. The main hazard if any non-severe storm could come to\nbe would be a quick burst of localized heavy rainfall and \ncloud to ground lightning. Meanwhile, smoke aloft departs \nslowly to the southeast with some blue skies returning into this\nafternoon across the Northland. Quiet conditions take over \ntonight as temperatures dip slightly below normal under light \nwinds. However, the next round of smoke aloft moves in tonight \nand sets up for Monday.\n\nThis incoming smoke layer will be creating sky cover for areas south\nof US Highway 2 throughout the day on Monday. Aside from the hazy\nskies, Monday will feature pleasant conditions with near-normal\ndaytime high temperatures climbing into the upper 70s to lower 80s\nacross the area. Humidity levels will drop a bit, creating a\ncomfortable feel for mid-August. Light variable winds in the morning\nwill eventually turn toward the west and northwest during the\nafternoon hours.\n\nHeading into the rest of the week, a persistent northwest flow aloft\ncontinues as a stalled frontal boundary to our south keeps us on\nthe cooler side. This northwest flow continues to create near- \nnormal daytime high temperatures in the 70s to lower 80s, and \nslightly below normal low temperatures each night falling into \nthe mid 40s to lower 50s.\n\nWithin this northwest flow will be weak and small-scale\ndisturbances helping create the off-and-on mainly rain shower \nwith embedded non-severe thunderstorm chances every 36 to 48 \nhours. Any showers and storms would likely be very localized and\nshort-lived in nature from this setup of shear, moisture and \ninstability-limited environmental progs in longer-range ensemble\nguidance.",
